+\subsection{An Intrinsically Rank-Two Boundary-Typed Target}
+\label{subsec:intrinsic-ranktwo}
+
+The next example makes the distinction between the rank-one and higher-rank
+quantitative regimes intrinsic to the language.  The lower bound is not tied
+to fan-out two: the target has no rule-rank-one MCFG presentation at
+\emph{any} finite fan-out.
+
+Let
+\[
+ \Sigma_{\mathrm{intr}}
+ :=\{\mathtt a,\mathtt b,\mathtt l,\mathtt r,\mathtt p,\mathtt q,
+       \mathtt c,\mathtt d,\mathtt{\#}\}
+\]
+and put
+\[
+ P_{\mathrm{intr}}
+ :=\{\mathtt{lpc},\mathtt{lpd},\mathtt{lqc},
+       \mathtt{rpc},\mathtt{rpd}\}.
+\]
+Define
+\[
+ \mathrm{MIX}_2^+
+ :=\{w\in\{\mathtt a,\mathtt b\}^+:
+       |w|_{\mathtt a}=|w|_{\mathtt b}\}
+\]
+and
+\[
+ L_{\mathrm{intr}}
+ :=P_{\mathrm{intr}}\,\mathtt{\#}\,\mathrm{MIX}_2^+.
+\]
+
+\begin{theorem}[An intrinsic-rank-two typed witness]
+\label{thm:intrinsic-ranktwo-witness}
+The language $L_{\mathrm{intr}}$ satisfies
+\[
+ L_{\mathrm{intr}}
+ \in
+ \Cmcf{1}{h_{\Sigma_{\mathrm{intr}}}^{1,1}}
+ \cap\mathsf{MCFL}(1,2),
+ \qquad
+ L_{\mathrm{intr}}\notin\YSub{1},
+\]
+and, for every finite $f'\ge1$,
+\[
+ L_{\mathrm{intr}}\notin\mathsf{MCFL}(f',1).
+\]
+Consequently its minimum rule rank among all finite-fan-out MCFG
+presentations is exactly two.  In particular,
+Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary-polydata} applies to a language that cannot be
+covered by Theorem~\ref{thm:rankone-polydata} at any fan-out.
+\end{theorem}
+
+\begin{proof}
+First, $L_{\mathrm{intr}}$ has a reduced good fan-out-one grammar of maximum
+rule rank two.  Let $B$ generate the nonempty balanced-count suffix by
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+ B\to{}&
+ \mathtt a B\mathtt b B
+ \mid \mathtt b B\mathtt a B
+ \mid \mathtt a B\mathtt b
+ \mid \mathtt a\mathtt b B
+ \mid \mathtt a\mathtt b\\
+ &{}\mid
+ \mathtt b B\mathtt a
+ \mid \mathtt b\mathtt a B
+ \mid \mathtt b\mathtt a .
+\end{aligned}
+\]
+The usual first-return induction on the difference
+$|w|_{\mathtt a}-|w|_{\mathtt b}$ shows that
+$L_B=\mathrm{MIX}_2^+$.  Add
+\[
+ S\to
+ \mathtt{lpc\#}B\mid
+ \mathtt{lpd\#}B\mid
+ \mathtt{lqc\#}B\mid
+ \mathtt{rpc\#}B\mid
+ \mathtt{rpd\#}B .
+\]
+This grammar is $\lambda$-free, linear, nondeleting, nonpermuting, and
+nonmerging, and has rule rank at most two.
+
+For typed membership, let
+\[
+ Q_{\mathrm{intr}}
+ :=P_{\mathrm{intr}}\,\mathtt{\#}\,\{\mathtt a,\mathtt b\}^+ .
+\]
+It is strictly $2$-local.  One set of allowed adjacent pairs, including end
+markers, is
+\[
+\begin{gathered}
+ \vdash\mathtt l,\ \vdash\mathtt r,\quad
+ \mathtt{lp},\ \mathtt{lq},\ \mathtt{rp},\quad
+ \mathtt{pc},\ \mathtt{pd},\ \mathtt{qc},\\
+ \mathtt{c\#},\ \mathtt{d\#},\quad
+ \mathtt{\#a},\ \mathtt{\#b},\quad
+ \mathtt{aa},\ \mathtt{ab},\ \mathtt{ba},\ \mathtt{bb},\quad
+ \mathtt a\dashv,\ \mathtt b\dashv .
+\end{gathered}
+\]
+Define the homomorphism
+$\varphi:\Sigma_{\mathrm{intr}}^*\to\mathbb Z$ by
+\[
+ \varphi(\mathtt a)=1,\qquad
+ \varphi(\mathtt b)=-1,
+\]
+and let every other terminal have image $0$.  Then
+\[
+ L_{\mathrm{intr}}
+ =Q_{\mathrm{intr}}\cap\varphi^{-1}(0).
+\]
+Lemma~\ref{lem:sl2-boundary-control} and
+Proposition~\ref{prop:abelian-coset-filter}(ii), together with the displayed
+CFG, give
+\[
+ L_{\mathrm{intr}}\in
+ \Cmcf{1}{h_{\Sigma_{\mathrm{intr}}}^{1,1}}.
+\]
+
+The language is outside Yoshinaka's class already at arity one.  Take
+\[
+ x:=\mathtt p,\qquad y:=\mathtt q,
+\]
+and one-hole contexts
+\[
+ E:=\mathtt l\blank_1\mathtt{c\#ab},
+ \qquad
+ F:=\mathtt r\blank_1\mathtt{d\#ab}.
+\]
+Then
+\[
+ E[x],\ E[y],\ F[x]\in L_{\mathrm{intr}},
+ \qquad
+ F[y]\notin L_{\mathrm{intr}}.
+\]
+Thus the substitution implication fails.  Notice that the boundary typing
+distinguishes the offending factors:
+$h_{\Sigma_{\mathrm{intr}}}^{1,1}(\mathtt p)
+ \ne h_{\Sigma_{\mathrm{intr}}}^{1,1}(\mathtt q)$.
+
+It remains to prove intrinsic rule rank.  Put
+\[
+ \mathrm{MIX}_2
+ :=\{w\in\{\mathtt a,\mathtt b\}^*:
+       |w|_{\mathtt a}=|w|_{\mathtt b}\}.
+\]
+Gallot calls an MCFG \emph{non-branching} when each positive-rank rule has a
+single child nonterminal; in the present terminology this is exactly rule
+rank at most one, with no globally fixed fan-out bound
+\cite[Definition~38]{Gallot2021}.  Since every finite grammar has a finite
+maximum nonterminal arity, Gallot's non-branching MCFL class is therefore
+\[
+ \bigcup_{f'<\infty}\mathsf{MCFL}(f',1).
+\]
+His proof of Theorem~31 rules out $\mathrm{MIX}_2$ from this class
+\cite[Propositions~5--6, Theorem~31]{Gallot2021}.
+The same language is the word problem of the infinite cyclic group in the
+two-letter presentation used by Bishop et al., who give a peer-reviewed
+expanded proof that it is not EDT0L
+\cite[Definition~7.1, Theorem~C]{BishopEtAl2026EDT0L}.
+
+Now let $\pi$ erase
+$\mathtt l,\mathtt r,\mathtt p,\mathtt q,\mathtt c,\mathtt d,\mathtt{\#}$
+and fix $\mathtt a,\mathtt b$.  Then
+\[
+ \pi(L_{\mathrm{intr}})=\mathrm{MIX}_2^+.
+\]
+If $L_{\mathrm{intr}}$ had a fan-out-$f'$ rule-rank-one presentation,
+applying $\pi$ to the terminal material in its rule templates would preserve
+fan-out and rule rank and would give such a presentation of
+$\mathrm{MIX}_2^+$.  Adding a fresh start symbol with one unary link to the
+old start and one rank-zero rule generating $\eps$ would then give a
+fan-out-$f'$ rule-rank-one presentation of $\mathrm{MIX}_2$, contradicting
+the non-branching lower bound.  Hence
+$L_{\mathrm{intr}}\notin\mathsf{MCFL}(f',1)$ for every finite $f'$.
+\end{proof}
+
+The non-branching lower bound is prior work.  What is specific to the present
+framework is that a strictly local wrapper and the $(1,1)$ boundary typing
+place the resulting context-free language inside the typed class while the
+displayed substitution rectangle keeps it outside Yoshinaka's trivial-typing
+baseline.
+
